{# Not a macro — a whole manifest. The renderer renders this file as `ingressroute.yaml`
   for every role that resolves `ingressroute.yaml` into its `manifests_files` and ships no
   `templates/ingressroute.yaml.j2` of its own. A role resolves it when its services
   entry carries a `hostname` and its include does not exclude it (`manifests_shared_defaults` in that role's
   defaults). This file is the wrapper around the `ingressroute()` macro below that each
   such role's IngressRoute template would otherwise repeat byte for byte.

   THE ENTRY IS THE INPUT. Name, host label, port, Authelia gate and public-route opt-out all
   come from the role's `services` entry. The entry is also where the deploy derives the
   role's traefik ordering edge from (`scripts/renderer/template_filters/toposort.py:build_k8s_dep_map`)
   — a hostname on the entry is what says "this role is routed", since the route itself leaves
   no file in the role.

   `public_route: false` on the entry renders the `.local.` name only, whatever
   `k8s_public_route` says. `scripts/docs/catalog_lib/route_facts.py:reachability` reads the
   same field, so the generated docs call the route LAN-only.

   A role whose route needs anything else — extra middlewares, a second hostname, a bypass
   prefix, a Jinja condition — keeps its own `templates/ingressroute.yaml.j2`, which wins over
   this file. The macro header in `k8s/templates/ingressroute.yml.j2` lists every parameter
   such a role would reach for. #}
{% from 'ingressroute.yml.j2' import ingressroute with context %}
---
{{ ingressroute(
    container_item.name,
    container_item.hostname | default(container_item.name),
    container_item.port,
    container_item.use_authelia,
    public=container_item.public_route | default(true),
  )
}}
